--- /dev/null
+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-21-reject-undefined-patch-value/proposal.md
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+## Why
+
+An RFC 6902 `value` member is a JSON value, and `undefined` is not one. `applyPatch` currently treats an operation whose `value` is `undefined` as having a value, so `add`/`replace` can produce documents containing `undefined`, and `test` compares against it. A review of `applyPatch` against RFC 6902 found this to be the only gap.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- `add`, `replace` and `test` operations whose `value` is `undefined` are rejected with a `JsonPatchError`, the same as a missing `value`.
+- Tests and a spec scenario cover this.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+- `json-patch`: validation of `value` rejects `undefined`.
+
+## Impact
+
+`src/json-patch.ts` (validation) and `src/json-patch.test.ts`. Callers passing `undefined` as a value now get an error instead of an invalid result.
diff --git a/openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-21-reject-undefined-patch-value/specs/json-patch/spec.md b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-10-21-reject-undefined-patch-value/specs/json-patch/spec.md
